WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:10.450
Many of the most important questions in public
policy are quantitative by nature, and government

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:14.839
organizations such as the Joint Committee
on Taxation and the Congressional Budget Office

00:00:14.839 --> 00:00:19.850
spend tens of millions of dollars developing
computational simulation models to address

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:25.420
those questions. As tax reform heats up, the
JCT will predict the revenue and macroeconomic

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.110
effects of competing proposals to guide Congress
and the public.

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:33.500
The CBO has had their share of the limelight
estimating the insurance coverage and budgetary

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:34.910
effects of health care reform.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:40.460
The problem is, the models these teams develop
are not available to the public. This undermines

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:45.010
the free competition of ideas because the
public can't use the models to develop creative,

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:46.010
new proposals.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:50.039
This undermines the predictive accuracy of
the models, because their creators can’t

00:00:50.039 --> 00:00:52.230
participate fully in the scientific community.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:56.609
And it undermines trust in government, because
how can the public trust that which resides

00:00:56.609 --> 00:00:57.769
behind a curtain?

00:00:57.769 --> 00:01:02.260
Congress could mandate that researchers in
government share all of their work openly,

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:08.200
and we would have better tax policy, better
spending policy, and a healthier democracy.

